        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Fit the first sensor in the sump pit, with its probe just above the level the pump normally clears, because that is the earliest warning you can get of the failure most likely to flood a basement. Then work outward: the water heater base, behind the washing machine, the furnace condensate, and the lowest point of the floor. Several cheap units beat one expensive one in the wrong place.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Mix the types deliberately. Smart sensors are the only thing that helps when the house is empty, and standalone buzzers are the only thing that still works when the storm takes the power, the router and the sump pump together. Both cases are real, the whole setup costs under a hundred dollars, and it is the one item on a waterproofing list that assumes everything else will eventually fail.
          </p>
        </div>

